        "heading": "3.1 TEXT-GUIDED STYLEGAN IMAGE MANIPULATION",
        "text": "StyleSpace of StyleGAN The generators of StyleGAN family (Karras et al., 2019; 2020a;b) have a number of latent spaces: Z , W , W+, and S. The original latent space is Z , which is typically the standard normal distribution. The generator transforms an input noise z \u223c N(0, I) into intermediate latent spaces W (Karras et al., 2019), W+ (Abdal et al., 2019), and S (Wu et al., 2021), sequentially. Recent study (Wu et al., 2021) shows that StyleSpace S is the most disentangled such that it can change a distinct visual attribute in a localized way. The number of style channels in S is 6048 excluding toRGB channels for StyleGAN-ADA (resolution 10242), and recent methods (Kocasari et al., 2022; Patashnik et al., 2021; Wu et al., 2021) modify the values of the channels to edit an image. Our method also adopts StyleSpace and we use k to denote the total number of channels in S (that is, s = [s1, s2, \u00b7 \u00b7 \u00b7 , sk] \u2208 S). For the convenience of explanation, the pre-trained generator G is re-defined with respect to StyleSpace as X = Gs(s); X is a generated image. Note that s is the inverted style vector of image X , found via StyleGAN inversion methods such as (Alaluf et al., 2021; Roich et al., 2021; Tov et al., 2021) used for manipulation purpose in most cases.\nText-guided Manipulation via StyleCLIP The goal of StyleGAN-based image manipulation is to find a direction s\u0302 = [s\u03021, s\u03022, ..., \u00b7 \u00b7 \u00b7 , s\u0302k] in StyleSpace S which generates an image Xedited = Gs(s+s\u0302) suitable for the provided text guidance t. Note that the main benefit of considering S is that it does not change the undesired regions of the image when modifying a small number of style channels by its well disentangled property.\nThe GlobalDirection (Patashnik et al., 2021) is a representative method that provides an inputagnostic manipulation as well as some level of control on disentanglement by pre-computing set of CLIP embeddings corresponding to every style channel in StyleSpace S. Specifically, StyleCLIP encodes the effect of manipulating i-th style channel using CLIP encoder E(\u00b7) \u2208 Rp (Radford et al., 2021) for all k channels to create the dictionary D \u2208 Rp\u00d7k (Fig. 1(b)), then derive the direction s\u0302 by the similarity between CLIP encoded text E(t) and D as follows:\ns\u0302 = E(t)TD \u2208 Rk. (1)\nStyleCLIP relies directly encodes the style channel individually to create the dictionary D := [d(\u00b1\u2206s1),d(\u00b1\u2206s2), \u00b7 \u00b7 \u00b7 ,d(\u00b1\u2206sk)] which is described in the Fig. 1(a). More specifically, d is an\noperation that encodes the change in image caused by the given direction \u2206s \u2208 Rk as follows: d(\u00b1\u2206s) := Es\u2208S [ E ( Gs(s+\u2206s) ) \u2212 E ( Gs(s\u2212\u2206s) )] (2)\nin which the expectation over style codes is approximately computed by training data in practice. Note that in GlobalDirection, the direction encoded by d is \u2206si = \u03c3ie(i), a standard basis vector whose i-th element \u03c3i is the standard deviation of the i-th channel, therefore d(\u00b1\u2206si) corresponds to the CLIP representation of the manipulation effect caused by i-th channel manipulation.\nIn the following section (Sec. 3.2), we propose the evidence to the hypothesis that the single-channel encoding strategy with \u2206si is the major bottleneck causing limited coverage issues that StyleCLIP suffers from.\n3.2 COVERAGE ANALYSIS OF STYLECLIP GLOBALDIRECTION METHOD\nText-guided discovery of global direction is ideally preferred over unsupervised methods due to its ability to manipulate a given image with wide versatility (corresponding to various text inputs) which enables finding not only the directions found by unsupervised methods but also previously undiscovered directions as well. However, in this section, we argue that contrary to our expectation on the versatility of text guidance, the GlobalDirection of StyleCLIP surprisingly fails to discover a large portion of the instances from unsupervised directions. Moreover, it even fails to represent the new directions guided via text, to the fullest capacity of style-based GAN models (Karras et al., 2019; 2020a;b; Sauer et al., 2022).\nTo this end, we design two experiments that serves as an evidence to the limited coverage of StyleCLIP GlobalDirection (Patashnik et al., 2021) on unsupervised and text-guided directions respectively2, using StyleGAN2-ADA (Karras et al., 2020a) trained on FFHQ dataset (Karras et al., 2019), image resolution of 1024.\nFigure 2: Manipulation results with randomly selected text. GlobalDirection is denoted as GD.\nAttractive Male Innocent Eyes\nOriginal GD Ours\nEvil Queen\nGrumpy Face\nShiny HairSexy Man\nOriginal GD Ours Original GD Ours\nIn Tab. 1, we measure the cosine similarity between d(\u00b1\u03b1) and E(Xedited) where the manipulation of image is performed with either GlobalDirection or unsupervised approach (Shen & Zhou, 2021; H\u00e4rk\u00f6nen et al., 2020). Unlike the original framework where the guidance is given as a CLIP-encoded text t, we substitute E(t) with d(\u00b1\u03b1), which conceptually is a CLIP representation of image change induced by the direction \u03b1. Since cos(d(\u00b1\u03b1), E(Xedited)) is constantly higher when using directions from unsupervised methods, it is evident that GlobalDirection is failing in recovery of unsupervised directions. Moreover, as shown in Fig. 2(b), despite the innate versatility of text, GlobalDirection shows inability in adapting to various text t. For additional results using randomly selected text prompts, please refer to Appendix A.\nRegarding the phenomena found in the above two experiments, we hypothesize that the failure of GlobalDirection is due to the crude assumption on the StyleSpace in which the inconsistency of sample-wise manipulation result intensifies when modifying a single channel with \u2206si. Such inconsistency harms the robust representation of i-th channel since d(\u00b1\u2206si) is the expectation of such inconsistent samples. Thereby we demonstrate that the basic assumption held by GlobalDirection which expects a single channel manipulation on any images to be semantically consistent in CLIP space, in fact, has numerous counter-examples showing inconsistency.\n2We performed the experiments here using the GlobalDirection method and test examples provided on the StyleCLIP official site: https://github.com/orpatashnik/StyleCLIP\nIn Fig. 3(a), the distribution of E ( Gs(s+\u2206si) ) \u2212E ( Gs(s\u2212\n\u2206si) )\nover s \u223c S is shown as an angle-histogram with red dashed line indicating the expectation d(\u00b1\u2206si) which is the i-th channel representation of GlobalDirection. As the samples show inconsistent angles over polar coordinate, the expectation is not a robust representative. Note that \u03b1(r) means a direction has r nonzero entries and k \u2212 r zero entries.\nOn the other hand, it is observable that manipulation by unsupervised direction \u03b1, with Fig. 3(b)50 and Fig. 3(c)100 channels respectively, shows great improvement in consistency hence yields robust expectation, d(\u00b1\u03b1). A more detailed explanation on representing the samples as directions for a angle histogram is deferred to Appendix B due to space constraints.\nMoreover, we show an example where single channel alone have completely different meaning from the manipulation by the combination of multiple channels in Fig. 4. This supports our claim that the interaction between multiple channels should be considered, rather than individually encoding single channel each. Due to the na\u00efve strategy of GlobalDirection, it fails to find the two channels (4520, 4426) for white hair when the text guidance is given as \u2018white hair\u2019."
