        "heading": "4 DICTIONARY LEARNING FOR SPARSE REPRESENTATION OF CLIP SPACE",
        "text": "As discussed in Sec. 3.2, each style channel rather than the whole may not be meaningfully embedded in CLIP space. To address this issue, we propose a dictionary learning approach to find a robust representation of styles, in other words channels, in StyleSpace.\nIdeally, we would like to construct a dictionary such that the manipulation direction s\u0302 obtained by E(t)TD (Eq. (1)) with learned dictionary should correspond to the given driving text t. To see this, let us for now assume that the \u2018ground truth\u2019 manipulation direction \u03b1t corresponding to the driving text t is given. Then our estimated direction using learned dictionary as in Eq. (1), \u03b1\u0302 = E(t)TD, should be close to the ground truth \u03b1t which leads to the first term of the loss:\nminimize D\n\u2225\u2225\u03b1t \u2212 E(t)TD\u2225\u222522 (3)\nwhich we name ideal dictionary learning problem since the ground truth direction \u03b1t for a driving text t is unknown. To avoid this, we substitute \u03b1t with the known directions \u03b1 derived from unsupervised methods (H\u00e4rk\u00f6nen et al., 2020; Shen & Zhou, 2021). Since E(t) still does not exist in the second term, we use \u03b1 itself for driving manipulation in the second term rather than using t, to reconstruct \u03b1 given our dictionary (for ideal \u03b1t, we have E(t) = d(\u00b1\u03b1t) and hence \u2225\u03b1t \u2212 d(\u00b1\u03b1t)TD\u2225). The remaining question is how we can construct the set of known directions \u03b1 in Eq. (3). As mentioned above, we use all known directions found by GANSpace and SeFa (H\u00e4rk\u00f6nen et al., 2020; Shen & Zhou, 2021). However, it turns out that na\u00efvely using them shows instability in terms of maintaining the original image, which is called entanglement issue. This is due to the innate properties of unsupervised methods that use principal component analysis or eigenvalue decomposition in the intermediate space of StyleGAN. The found style directions do not have zero entries, which indicates that all k channels are modified by the direction \u03b1 in S . As can be seen in the experimental section, we empirically found that problems such as extreme entangling or image collapse occur when more than 300 coordinates of style direction \u03b1 are simultaneously activated.\nTo remedy this, we constraint \u03b1 to have impact on a limited number of styles. Specifically, we simply prune styles that have small magnitudes based on the ranking of coordinates, hence we have sparsified direction \u03b1 \u2297m where mj = 1 if \u03b1j \u2208 Top\u2212 r(\u03b1) and 0 otherwise, Top\u2212 r(\u03b1) is the set of top r coordinates of \u03b1 in magnitude and \u2297 indicates element-wise multiplication. This sparsified direction guarantees only TOP-r style channels to be modified while other channels relevant to unwanted local deformation stay intact.\nArmed with the sparsified version of the directions \u03b1(r), where r is the number of non-zero entries, we introduce additional loss term \u2225d(\u00b1\u03b1t) \u2212 D\u03b1t\u222522 that encourages disentanglement in image manipulation to equation 3. Since we use 10, 30, ad 50 non-zero entries for each direction, each direction \u03b1 has three versions of itself, \u03b110, \u03b130 and \u03b150. Since the combination of overlapping and non-overlapping channels are simultaneously introduces in the dictionary learning process as a linear combination, the representation for each channel could be refined to have less entanglement. In Appendix C, we informally explain about the details of how our objective using sparsified \u03b1 set encourages to learn disentangled bases of D. Hence our method may have greatly disentangled and diverse directions compared to the unsupervised directions (even though our \u03b1 comes from the unsupervised methods).\nCombining these two ingredients together yields our dictionary learning problem by considering all known directions \u03b1:\nminimize D \u2211 \u03b1 \u2225\u2225\u03b1 \u2212 d(\u00b1\u03b1)TD\u2225\u22252 2 + \u03bb \u00b7 \u2225\u2225D\u03b1 \u2212 d(\u00b1\u03b1)\u2225\u22252 2 . (4)\nwhere \u03bb is the tunable hyper-parameter set as 0.01 and \u03b1t for ideal objective is substituted by unsupervised directions \u03b1."
