        "heading": "5 EXPERIMENTS",
        "text": "We evaluate the proposed method on StyleGAN2 (Karras et al., 2020a). We used StyleGAN2-ADA pretrained on multiple datasets, including FFHQ (Karras et al., 2019), LSUN (Yu et al., 2015) Car,\nTable 3: Cosine similarity RCLIP measured in CLIP Space.\nChurch, AFHQ (Choi et al., 2020) Dog, Cat. In this section, we show the widened StyleGAN manipulation coverage of our method compared to a text-guided method, GlobalDirection of StyleCLIP (Patashnik et al., 2021) mainly on FFHQ pre-trained model. For additional details in experimental design, please refer to Appendix E.\nQuantitative Results We measure the superiority of our method in terms of recovering the unsupervised directions \u03b1 and text guidance t. The metrics are defined in StyleSpace S to evaluate over unsupervised directions and CLIP space for evaluation over text guidance. In order to assess is Multi2One recover the unsupervised direction with maximum manipulative ability and minimum entanglement, we define three metrics as follows.\n\u2022 StyleSpace metric for unsupervised directions: As a measure for discrepancy existent between GlobalDirection and unsupervised directions, we employ the following metric:\nMSE(\u03b1, \u03b1\u0302) := E\u03b1\u223cA [ \u2225\u03b1\u0302 \u2212\u03b1\u222522 ] (5)\nwhere \u03b1 is from a set of unsupervised directions A and the reconstruction of such direction is given by \u03b1\u0302 = d(\u00b1\u03b1)TD in which the text guidance E(t) is replaced with d(\u00b1\u03b1), following the standard framework described in Sec. 3.1. Conceptually, d(\u00b1\u03b1) is a CLIP representation of change in images caused by manipulation with the direction \u03b1, computed in the same exact manner of Eq. (2). Tab. 2 shows that Multi2One consistently outperforms GlobalDirection in terms of cosine similarity between the unsupervised direction and the estimated direction. MSE ( \u03b1, \u03b1\u0302 \u00b7 I{R \u0338=0}(\u03b1) ) indicate the non-zero entries of ground truth direction \u03b1 which could be used for measuring the pure manipulation ability by computing the error in channels that should be modified. On the other hand, MSE ( \u03b1,\n\u03b1\u0302 \u00b7 I{0}(\u03b1) )\nis a metric used for calculating the entanglement that happened in channels that should not be modified.\n\u2022 CLIP space metric for unknown directions: We measure the similarity between edited image and the text guidance in CLIP space as follows:\nRCLIP(t,Xedited) := Et\u223cT [ cos ( E(t), E(Xedited) )] . (6)\nWe denote T, the set of text prompts that describes the pre-trained domain of StyleGAN, namely FFHQ. Tab. 3 shows that the cosine similarity between given text and the edited image is consistently higher using our method, Multi2One. Top\u2212 r indicates the number of channels that are manipulated in StyleSpace.\nQualitative Results We demonstrate the effectiveness of our method, compared to the state-ofthe-arts method, GlobalDirection. We conducted all experiments using the pretrained models and the precomputed set of CLIP embeddings, d(\u2206si), that are readily provided for FFHQ, AFHQ Cat and AFHQ Dog. We computed the CLIP embeddings of the StyleSpace of LSUN Church and Car using the same configuration with GlobalDirection. All the results are compared under same condition, where manipulation strength and disentanglement levels are set to be equal. More specifically, we modify same number of channels for both methods and the magnitude of change3 in StyleSpace is always fixed as 10.\n3The original paper StyleCLIP refers to the magnitude of change in StyleSpace as \u03b1 and the disentanglement level as \u03b2 which we substitute by number of channel that are changed.\nThe manipulated images show that GlobalDirection fails to represent some of the most basic text such as \u2018Young\u2019 for FFHQ. On the other hand, ours successfully manipulates the images not only on the basic text guidances but also on advanced semantics such as \u2018Little mermaid\u2019 and \u2018Joker smile\u2019 for FFHQ. We emphasize that despite the dictionary of our method is learned from the known directions in unsupervised approaches (Shen & Zhou, 2021; H\u00e4rk\u00f6nen et al., 2020), the manipulation results show that our learned dictionary could adapt to previously unseen combination of semantics such as red hair, pale skin, and big eyes to represent \u2018Little Mermaid\u2019 and unnatural smiles with red lipstick and pale face to represent \u2018Joker smile\u2019.\nWe show in Fig. 8 that the unsupervised direction \u03b1 whose CLIP representation d(\u00b1\u03b1) has the maximum cosine similarity with the CLIP encoding of text \u2018Little Mermaid\u2019 and \u2018Joker smile\u2019 have slightly different direction compared to the manipulation result in Sec. 5. For t =\u2018Little mermaid\u2019, among the 1440 directions that we used for dictionary learning, the direction that scored the highest cosine similarity of 0.1094 with E(t) was the 1475th direction. As shown in Fig. 8, the direction has little relevance with the given text. Also, given a text \u2018Joker smile\u2019, the direction that scored highest cosine similarity of 0.1901 was 316th whose application to StyleSpace shows exaggerated smiles but not with red lipsticks and pale skin. We show additional results of our method compared to\nGlobalDirection based on AFHQ(Sec. 5) and LSUN(Sec. 5) dataset on which StyleGAN-ADA is pretrained. Similar to FFHQ, we observed that styleCLIP fails to manipulated given images with simple text like \u2018Black fur\u2019 for AFHQ-dog and \u2018Brick church\u2019 for LSUN-church. We demonstrate the superiority of ours which discovers \u2018Blue fur\u2019 from AFHQ-dog pretrained StyleGAN and \u2018Golden church\u2019 from LSUN-church pretrained StyleGAN.\nREPRODUCIBILITY STATEMENT\nWe will release our code and the implementation details to be publicly available for reproducibility."
